\documentclass[11pt]{book}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\title{Notes on Field Botany}
\author{E. Rivera}
\date{2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\chapter{Preface}
These notes grew out of three summers of fieldwork in the Serrania de
Ronda. They are written for students who already know the major plant
families and want practice reading landscapes rather than specimens.

\tableofcontents

\chapter{Habitats}
Wetlands reward patience: the same square metre visited in April, June
and September is three different places.
\section{Wetlands}
Look first at the water line, then at the sedges, and only then at the
showier flowers.
\section{Dry Slopes}
South-facing slopes hold the therophyte communities, and the shortest
turf is often the richest.

\chapter{Identification}
Keys are a starting point, not an answer; a keyed-out name still has to
survive comparison with a full description.
\section{Reading a Key}
Work through couplets in order and never skip a character you cannot
score on your specimen.

\chapter{Using Keys}
A key works by elimination, so one uncertain couplet can send the whole
determination down the wrong branch.
\section{Common Pitfalls}
The classic pitfall is scoring a damaged specimen as if it were intact.

\chapter{After the Season}
Pressing and mounting notes, label conventions, and the herbarium
handover close the season's work.
\end{document}

